IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `gas-types`

-- Gas Act 1986 (UK) — GOAL 2: CUTTING OFF FOR DEBT, AND ENTRY (Schedule 2B
-- paras 7, 11, 19, 24 and 28(1)).
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1986-44.txt.
-- The consumer asks: "Can my supplier cut off my gas or fit a prepayment
-- meter for an unpaid bill or deposit, from when, and may its officer enter;
-- can I restore it myself; can it refuse to restore it?"

§ `Goal 2 — cutting off for debt`

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` n `days after` d MEANS Date (Day d PLUS n)

-- A payment or deposit counts as made before a day only if made on an
-- earlier day.
GIVEN m IS A MAYBE DATE, d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `made before` m `the day` d IF
    CONSIDER m
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST x  THEN x LESS THAN d

-- para 7(1): "(a) a demand in writing is made by a gas supplier for any of
-- the relevant payments ...; and (b) the consumer does not make those
-- payments within 28 days after the making of the demand." Payment by the
-- demand day + 28 is in time, so the sub-paragraph applies from the demand
-- day + 29. (5): not for an amount "genuinely in dispute".
@ref Sch 2B para 7(1), (5)
GIVEN p IS A `Payment demand`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day para 7(1) applies from for` p MEANS
    IF     p's `genuinely in dispute`
        OR `made before` (p's `paid on`) `the day` (`the day` 29 `days after` (p's `demand made on`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the day` 29 `days after` (p's `demand made on`))

-- para 7(2): "(a) a request in writing is made ... for the provision of a
-- deposit ...; and (b) the consumer does not provide such a deposit, or agree
-- to take his supply through a pre-payment meter, within 7 days after the
-- making of the request." Applies from the request day + 8.
@ref Sch 2B para 7(2), (5)
GIVEN q IS A `Deposit request`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day para 7(2) applies from for` q MEANS
    IF     q's `genuinely in dispute`
        OR `made before` (q's `deposit given or prepayment agreed on`) `the day` (`the day` 8 `days after` (q's `request made on`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the day` 8 `days after` (q's `request made on`))

-- The power arises on the earlier of the two days. A payment, deposit or
-- prepayment agreement made after the 28 (or 7) days does not end it: para
-- 7(1)(b) and (2)(b) are met once the time has passed (F3, changed after the
-- independent pass).
@ref Sch 2B para 7(1)-(2)
GIVEN c IS A `Cut-off case`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day the power arises for` c MEANS
    `the earlier of the two` fromDemand fromDeposit
    WHERE
        fromDemand MEANS
            CONSIDER c's `the demand`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST p  THEN `the day para 7(1) applies from for` p
        fromDeposit MEANS
            CONSIDER c's `the deposit request`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST q  THEN `the day para 7(2) applies from for` q

GIVEN a IS A MAYBE DATE, b IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the earlier of the two` a b MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN NOTHING THEN b
    WHEN JUST x  THEN
        CONSIDER b
        WHEN NOTHING THEN a
        WHEN JUST y  THEN JUST (`the earlier of` x y)

-- para 7(3)-(4): only a relevant supplier (one supplying the premises now),
-- or the current supplier to whom the debt has been assigned.
@ref Sch 2B para 7(3)-(4)
GIVEN c IS A `Cut-off case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supplier acting is entitled under para 7(3) or (4)` c IF
        c's `the supplier is my current supplier`
    OR  c's `the debt was assigned to my current supplier`

-- para 7(3): "after giving not less than 7 days' notice of his intention".
-- The notice must be given on or after the day the power arises; the
-- earliest day to act is the notice day + 7 (F2).
@ref Sch 2B para 7(3)
@export From what day may my gas supplier cut off my supply or fit a prepayment meter for an unpaid bill or refused deposit, under Schedule 2B para 7 of the Gas Act 1986 (UK)? The power arises if a written demand is not paid within 28 days after it is made, or a written deposit request is not met within 7 days after it is made, and not for an amount genuinely in dispute; it is exercisable by my current supplier (or the current supplier to whom the debt is assigned) after not less than 7 days' notice. NOTHING where there is no such power or no notice given on or after it arose. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Cut-off case`  @desc The demand or deposit request and their dates, the supplier, the notice of intention, and the day of the cut-off
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the earliest day my supply can be cut off for` c MEANS
    IF NOT (`the supplier acting is entitled under para 7(3) or (4)` c)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        CONSIDER `the day the power arises for` c
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST a  THEN
            CONSIDER c's `notice of intention given on`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST n  THEN
                IF n AT LEAST a
                THEN JUST (`the day` 7 `days after` n)
                ELSE NOTHING

@ref Sch 2B para 7(3)
@export May my gas supplier cut off my supply or fit a prepayment meter on the day it did (or proposes to), under Schedule 2B para 7 of the Gas Act 1986 (UK), and so recover its expenses from me? DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Cut-off case`  @desc The demand or deposit request and their dates, the supplier, the notice of intention, and the day of the cut-off
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `my supply may be cut off on the day for` c IF
    CONSIDER `the earliest day my supply can be cut off for` c
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST e  THEN c's `cut off or meter fitted on` AT LEAST e

§§ `Restoring the supply (paras 11, 19)`

-- para 11(1)-(2): no person shall, without the relevant consent, restore the
-- supply; doing so is an offence.
@ref Sch 2B para 11
@export Would restoring my gas supply myself be an offence under Schedule 2B para 11 of the Gas Act 1986 (UK)? Yes, unless the supplier who cut it off (or a supplier who is or is about to become my supplier) consents. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN r IS A `Restoration facts`  @desc Whether a supplier has consented to the supply being restored
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `restoring the supply myself is an offence for` r IF
    NOT (r's `the supplier's consent to restore`)

-- para 19(1)(b), (2): after a cut-off under para 7(3) or (4), the supplier
-- need not resume the supply until the consumer is no longer an owner or
-- occupier, or has made good the default and paid the reasonable expenses.
@ref Sch 2B para 19
@export Can my gas supplier refuse to restore my supply after cutting it off for debt, under Schedule 2B para 19 of the Gas Act 1986 (UK)? Yes, after a cut-off lawful under para 7(3) or (4), until I have made good the default and paid the reasonable expenses of cutting off and restoring, unless I am no longer the owner or occupier. Para 19 gives no such right after a cut-off that para 7 did not allow. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Cut-off case`        @desc The cut-off facts
      r IS A `Restoration facts`   @desc Whether I have made good the default, paid the expenses, or left the premises
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supplier may refuse to restore for` c `with` r IF
        `my supply may be cut off on the day for` c
    AND NOT (    r's `no longer the owner or occupier`
             OR (r's `default made good` AND r's `reasonable expenses paid`))

§§ `Entry to cut off (paras 24, 28(1))`

-- para 24(1)(a), (2): where the supplier is authorised to cut off the
-- supply, its officer may, "after 24 hours' notice to the occupier", at all
-- reasonable times, on production of an authenticated document, enter to cut
-- off the supply. para 28(1)(a): only if the supplier took reasonable steps
-- to ensure the officer is a fit and proper person.
@ref Sch 2B paras 24(1)(a), (2), 28(1)(a)
@export May my gas supplier's officer enter my premises to cut off the supply, under Schedule 2B paras 24 and 28 of the Gas Act 1986 (UK)? Only if the supplier may cut off the supply, after at least 24 hours' notice, at a reasonable time, on production of a document showing the officer's authority, and if the supplier has taken reasonable steps to ensure the officer is fit and proper. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Cut-off case`  @desc The cut-off facts
      e IS A `Entry facts`   @desc The notice given in hours, the time, the officer's document, and the supplier's checks on the officer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may enter to cut off for` c `with` e IF
        `my supply may be cut off on the day for` c
    AND e's `hours of notice given` AT LEAST 24
    AND e's `at a reasonable time`
    AND e's `authority document produced`
    AND e's `reasonable steps taken that the officer is fit and proper`
